```

```{r}
# Import and clean the Professor Trash Wheel data
professor_trash =
  read_excel(
    "data/202610 Trash Wheel Collection Data.xlsx",
    sheet = "Professor Trash Wheel",
    range = "A2:M140",
    na = c("", "dive")
  ) |>
  clean_names() |>
  filter(!is.na(dumpster)) |>
  mutate(
    year = as.integer(year),
    trash_wheel = "Professor Trash Wheel"
  )
```

```{r}
# Import and clean the Gwynnda data
gwynnda_trash =
  read_excel(
    "data/202610 Trash Wheel Collection Data.xlsx",
    sheet = "Gwynnda the Good Wheel of the W",
    range = "A2:L395"
  ) |>
  clean_names() |>
  filter(!is.na(dumpster)) |>
  mutate(
    year = as.integer(year),
    trash_wheel = "Gwynnda"
  )
```

```{r}
# Combine the three Trash Wheel datasets
trash_wheel_data =
  bind_rows(
    mr_trash,
    professor_trash,
    gwynnda_trash
  )
```

```{r}
# Calculate the total weight collected by Professor Trash Wheel
professor_total_weight =
  trash_wheel_data |>
  filter(trash_wheel == "Professor Trash Wheel") |>
  summarize(
    total_weight = sum(weight_tons, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |>
  pull(total_weight)

# Calculate cigarette butts collected by Gwynnda in June 2022
gwynnda_june_2022 =
  trash_wheel_data |>
  filter(
    trash_wheel == "Gwynnda",
    year == 2022,
    month == "June"
  ) |>
  summarize(
    total_cigarette_butts = sum(cigarette_butts, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |>
  pull(total_cigarette_butts)
```

The combined Trash Wheel dataset contains `r nrow(trash_wheel_data)` observations and `r ncol(trash_wheel_data)` variables from Mr. Trash Wheel, Professor Trash Wheel, and Gwynnda. Key variables include dumpster number, month and year of collection, trash weight and volume, and counts of different types of debris such as plastic bottles, polystyrene, cigarette butts, plastic bags, and wrappers. For the available data, Professor Trash Wheel collected a total of `r professor_total_weight` tons of trash. Gwynnda collected a total of `r format(gwynnda_june_2022, big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)` cigarette butts in June 2022.


## Problem 3

```{r, message=FALSE}
# Import and clean the baseline demographic data
baseline_data =
  read_csv(
    "data/MCI_baseline.csv",
    skip = 1,
    na = "."
  ) |>
  clean_names() |>
  mutate(
    sex = factor(
      sex,
      levels = c(0, 1),
      labels = c("Female", "Male")
    ),
    apoe4 = factor(
      apoe4,
      levels = c(0, 1),
      labels = c("Non-carrier", "Carrier")
    )
  ) |>
  filter(
    is.na(age_at_onset) | age_at_onset > current_age
  )
```

```{r}
# Count recruited participants
nrow(baseline_data)

# Count participants who developed MCI during follow-up
baseline_data |>
  summarize(
    developed_mci = sum(!is.na(age_at_onset))
  )

# Calculate the average baseline age
baseline_data |>
  summarize(
    mean_baseline_age = mean(current_age)
  )

# Calculate the proportion of women who are APOE4 carriers
baseline_data |>
  filter(sex == "Female") |>
  summarize(
    proportion_apoe4_carrier = mean(apoe4 == "Carrier")
  )
```

The baseline demographic data contain participant ID, baseline age, sex, education, APOE4 carrier status, and age at MCI onset. During import, I skipped the first descriptive row, treated "." as missing, converted sex and APOE4 carrier status to factors, and removed participants whose MCI onset occurred at or before their baseline age. After applying these inclusion criteria, 479 participants remained. Among them, 93 developed MCI during follow-up. The average baseline age was approximately 65.03 years, and 30% of women were APOE4 carriers.

```{r, message=FALSE}
# Import and tidy the longitudinal amyloid data
amyloid_data =
  read_csv(
    "data/mci_amyloid.csv",
    skip = 1,
    na = c(".", "NA", "Na")
  ) |>
  clean_names() |>
  pivot_longer(
    cols = -study_id,
    names_to = "visit",
    values_to = "amyloid_ratio"
  ) |>
  rename(
    id = study_id
  ) |>
  mutate(
    amyloid_ratio = as.numeric(amyloid_ratio)
  ) |>
  filter(
    !is.na(amyloid_ratio)
  )
```

The amyloid dataset contains repeated measurements of the amyloid β42/40 ratio for each participant at baseline and follow-up visits. During import, I skipped the descriptive header row, treated ".", "NA", and "Na" as missing values, reshaped the data from wide to long format, converted the amyloid ratio to numeric, and removed observations with missing biomarker measurements.

```{r}
# Find participants who appear only in the baseline dataset
baseline_only =
  baseline_data |>
  anti_join(
    amyloid_data |>
      distinct(id),
    by = "id"
  )

# Find participants who appear only in the amyloid dataset
amyloid_only =
  amyloid_data |>
  distinct(id) |>
  anti_join(
    baseline_data,
    by = "id"
  )

nrow(baseline_only)
nrow(amyloid_only)
```

There were 8 participants who appeared only in the baseline dataset and 16 participants who appeared only in the amyloid dataset. I therefore used an inner join to retain only participants present in both datasets.

```{r}
# Combine baseline and amyloid data, keeping participants in both datasets
combined_mci_data =
  inner_join(
    baseline_data,
    amyloid_data,
    by = "id"
  )

# Export the combined dataset
write_csv(
  combined_mci_data,
  "data/mci_combined.csv"
)
```

After retaining only participants present in both datasets, the combined longitudinal dataset contains 471 participants, 2188 observations, and 8 variables. Each observation represents a participant-specific biomarker measurement at a particular visit, along with the participant's baseline demographic and clinical information.


